<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="026" project="spaarke-ontology-platform-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Rule-body describer: generate the plain-language condition, read-side</title>
    <phase>2b Rule grammar (D-16)</phase>
    <gate>024 complete</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>New BFF code whose output is shown to users as the statement of what a rule tested; a wrong description is a section 0.3 failure.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Deterministic mapping over a closed grammar.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <deps>024</deps>
    <tags>bff-api,signals,rule-grammar</tags>
    <estimated-effort>3-4 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Build a read-side describer that turns a compiled rule body into plain-language clauses (GUIDs resolved to category names), used by the wizard's "How this was determined" and the admin rule page; generate it, never store it.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE backend developer. The words describe exactly what the filter tests, nothing more.</role>
  <goal>For every body the compiler accepts, the describer returns one plain-language line per clause plus the formal form, deterministically, with GUIDs shown as names.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>v4 shows "How this was determined" with each clause in words and in formal form (v4-prototype-vs-solution #11); sprk_sentence is plain text and no column holds a plain-language clause; rule bodies hold GUIDs. #11 recommends a small read-side describer in the BFF: generate, do not store. The v4 baseline (owner, 2026-10-05) requires the disclosure in the wizard's What was found step (reconciliation Z-2, W-6) and the admin rule page shows the condition in words (M-3).</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="new">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Signals/RuleBodyDescriber.cs</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Signals/PredicateCompiler.cs</file>
      <file role="new">tests/unit/domain/Signals/RuleBodyDescriberTests.cs</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the describer reads the SAME parsed body the compiler compiles (one parse), so the words cannot drift from the filter. It never reads data rows: lookup GUIDs are resolved to names by one batched read of the referenced reference table (e.g. sprk_triagecategory), as the evaluator principal.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: covers exactly the shapes the compiler accepts at the time it runs (Existence incl. task 024's subject-only shapes; Threshold if task 025 has landed). An unknown shape returns a refusal, never a guess.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: a classifier-based clause is described as a classification ("was classified as Fee / rate change"), per v4 HANDOFF section 1.4 L116 (binding).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: no endpoint in this task; consumers (058 via the 036 plan/read endpoint, 100) call the service. No new Dataverse column.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: if this task changes src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api, state the Placement Justification in the PR citing .claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md, and measure publish size against a FRESH build of master from a short path with file counts compared on both sides. Ceiling 60 MB.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests asserting category names must be paired with a seam read of the real sprk_triagecategory rows.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>Rule grammar</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarke-ontology-platform-r1/notes/v4-prototype-vs-solution.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarke-ontology-platform-r1/notes/pathb-fetchxml-reference.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarke-ontology-platform-r1/spec.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Declare the rigor level and reason per root CLAUDE.md section 8, then load the knowledge files above before touching code.</step>
    <step order="1">Expose the compiler's parsed body (or a shared parse) so the describer and compiler read one representation.</step>
    <step order="2">Implement RuleBodyDescriber: per clause a plain-language line, the formal form and the clause id that evidence lines (FR-48) cite.</step>
    <step order="3">Tests: Path B v2 body, the three Do fixtures from task 024, a refused shape.</step>
    <step order="4">Update tasks/TASK-INDEX.md: set this task to the completed marker, AND set this POML status element to completed. Both, or scripts/check-task-status-drift.ps1 fails the next push.</step>
    <step order="5">Record any deviation from this POML in projects/spaarke-ontology-platform-r1/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If a clause cannot be described without implying something the filter does not test (for example a comparison the body does not make), STOP and escalate with the clause rather than softening the wording.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>None found: grep for "Describe" in Services/Signals returns no describer; sprk_sentence is the rule's literal template, not a per-clause description.</existing>
    <extension>No: the compiler's job is FetchXML; mixing prose generation into it would give it a second reason to change. The describer reuses the compiler's parse.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>The wizard's "How this was determined" and the admin rule page have no data behind them (#11), so they would either be omitted or written by hand per rule and drift from the filter.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <tools>
    <tool name="dotnet">Build and test .NET</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Signals/RuleBodyDescriber.cs</output>
    <output type="test">tests/unit/domain/Signals/RuleBodyDescriberTests.cs</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given the Path B v2 body, when described, then it returns two clause lines, the first phrased as a classification naming the categories by name, the second stating no budget revision in the window, each with its formal form and clause id.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given each of task 024's three Do fixtures, when described, then each returns one line naming the subject, the date column and the relative date.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: given a body the compiler refuses, when described, then the describer refuses too.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: no Dataverse column was added and no description is persisted.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Tests cover the shapes above; anything beyond needs a one-line justification in task notes. All tests pass; the BFF builds.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>spec.md FR-48, FR-55, FR-57; #11. Off the critical path to 031.</notes>

  <completion date="2026-10-07">
    <summary>RuleBodyDescriber: gated on PolicyVersionValidator, same strict parse, one line per clause (words, formal form, clause id), lookup GUIDs shown as names by one batched read per reference table, nothing stored. Wired into the 036 response as ruleDescription. 13 domain tests + 2 live seam tests (passed against spaarkedev1). Choice option values print raw (known gap, see record). Publish +0.03 MB total vs fresh master (36.25). Commit 421da7944.</summary>
    <record>projects/spaarke-ontology-platform-r1/notes/036-026-stream-c-record.md</record>
  </completion>

  <execution><skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill></execution>
</task>
